=> Binary Trees

    -> Binary Tree
        -> A tree data structure in which each node can have at most two children.
        -> The two child positions are called Left Child and Right Child.
        -> A node may have 0, 1, or 2 children.

    -> Left and Right Children
        -> Left Child: A node connected to the left child position of its parent.
        -> Right Child: A node connected to the right child position of its parent.
        -> An ordinary Binary Tree does not require left values to be smaller than right values.

    -> Binary Search Tree (BST)
        -> A Binary Tree that follows a specific ordering rule.
        -> All values in the left subtree must be smaller than the current node's value.
        -> All values in the right subtree must be greater than the current node's value.
        -> The same ordering rule applies recursively to every subtree.
        -> This example assumes unique keys.

    -> Example BST
        ->           40
        ->          /  \
        ->         20   60
        ->        / \   / \
        ->       10 30 50 70
        -> Root = 40
        -> Left subtree values = 10, 20, 30
        -> Right subtree values = 50, 60, 70

    -> Searching in BST
        -> Start searching from the root.
        -> If search value equals the current node, the key is found.
        -> If search value is smaller, move to the left child.
        -> If search value is greater, move to the right child.
        -> Repeat until found or a null child is reached.
        -> Example: Search 50 => 40 -> 60 -> 50.

    -> Insertion in BST
        -> Start from the root and compare the new value.
        -> Smaller value => move left; Greater value => move right.
        -> Insert the new node at the first suitable empty child position.
        -> Example: Insert 25 => 40 -> 20 -> 30 -> Left of 30.
        -> This example uses unique values; duplicate-key handling depends on implementation.

    -> Deletion in BST
        -> Case 1: Leaf node => Remove the node directly.
        -> Case 2: Node with one child => Replace the node with its child.
        -> Case 3: Node with two children => Replace its key with the inorder successor or predecessor, then remove that replacement key from its original position.
        -> Inorder successor = Smallest key in the right subtree.
        -> Inorder predecessor = Largest key in the left subtree.
        -> All operations must preserve the BST ordering property.

    -> Balanced vs Unbalanced BST
        -> Balanced BST: Tree height remains O(log N).
        -> Unbalanced BST: Height can grow to O(N).
        -> Example: Inserting 10, 20, 30, 40 into a basic BST produces a right-skewed chain.
        -> Self-balancing BST examples include AVL Tree and Red-Black Tree.

    -> Time Complexity
        -> Search = O(h).
        -> Insertion = O(h).
        -> Deletion = O(h).
        -> h = Height of the tree.
        -> Balanced BST => O(log N) for all three operations.
        -> Worst-case unbalanced BST => O(N) for all three operations.

    -> Limitations of BST for Disk-Based Databases
        -> Each BST node normally contains one search key.
        -> A binary node has at most two child pointers.
        -> Large datasets can require many node visits.
        -> If nodes occupy separate disk pages, many visits can require costly page reads.
        -> Database systems favor wider trees that store many keys per node or page.
        -> B-Trees and B+ Trees can achieve large fan-out and smaller height.
        -> InnoDB commonly uses B+ Tree indexes to efficiently locate records and process ranges.

    -> Practical Java and MySQL Connection
        -> A Java BST can hold searchable values in memory.
        -> Java TreeMap and TreeSet are implemented using Red-Black Trees, not ordinary BSTs.
        -> MySQL InnoDB normally uses B+ Trees for clustered and secondary indexes.
        -> A B+ Tree node can correspond conceptually to a database page containing many keys.
        -> This reduces the number of page levels visited during database searches.